<InfoPage
  title="Editorial and Data Policy"
  description="How Modern Men's Fitness makes its numbers and content: where the standards data comes from, how calculators are built and sourced, how AI is used, and how corrections work."
  canonical="https://modernmensfitness.com/editorial-policy/"
  heading="Editorial and Data Policy"
  updated="2026-11-10"
>
  <p>Most fitness pages publish numbers with no source. This page explains how the numbers and content on this site are made, so you can judge how far to trust them.</p>

  <h2>Who is behind the site</h2>
  <p>Modern Men's Fitness is run by <a href="/about-jordan-hoppel/">Jordan Hoppel</a>, a strength coach in Scottsdale, Arizona, who has coached more than 1,300 athletes over 17 years.</p>

  <h2>How content is made</h2>
  <p>The calculators, data tables and many of the guides on this site are built with the help of AI tools and software. Jordan Hoppel directs the content and approves updates before they are published. We say this plainly because it affects how you should read the site: every claim that matters is meant to be checkable against a source we name, not taken on our authority.</p>

  <h2>Where the standards numbers come from</h2>
  <p>The <a href="/bench-press-standards/">bench press standards</a> are percentiles calculated from the public <a href="https://www.openpowerlifting.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenPowerlifting</a> database, which is released into the public domain. The page states exactly which lifters were included, how weight classes were set, and what the data does not describe. The working benchmarks for people who do not compete are labelled as estimates, not measured data.</p>

  <h2>How the calculators are built</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Each calculator shows its formula and names its source on the page.</li>
    <li>When a calculator is built or changed, its maths is checked against values worked out by hand.</li>
    <li>Where research gives a range or disagrees, the page says so instead of picking one number.</li>
    <li>If a claim cannot be supported by a source we can name, we leave it out.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Estimates and their limits</h2>
  <p>Calculators estimate. A calorie target, a body fat percentage or a one-rep max can be off for any one person, and each page explains by how much and why. Use the results as a starting point and adjust to what actually happens to you.</p>

  <h2>Updates and corrections</h2>
  <p>Pages show the date they were last updated. When new data or research changes a number, the page is updated. If you find an error, <a href="/contact/">tell us</a> with the page address and what looks wrong. We check every report and correct confirmed mistakes.</p>

  <h2>Advertising and products</h2>
  <p>This site may show advertising. Ads do not decide what the site says or which numbers it publishes. Jordan Hoppel also sells the Primal Press Protocol, a 12 week bench press program, and links to it from many pages. Those links are promotion for his own product.</p>

  <h2>Medical information</h2>
  <p>Nothing here is medical advice. See the <a href="/terms-of-use/">terms of use</a>.</p>
</InfoPage>
